  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sexual Health Services (SHASHA)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Zimbabwe):  USAID&apos;s Sexual Health Services (SHASHA) mechanism increases access to comprehensive, voluntary, high-quality, family planning (FP) services among poor, underserved, and hard-to-reach populations with a particular focus on adolescents, young women (AGYW), and people with disabilities (PWD) across all ten provinces.. The mechanism is guided by two main objectives:.Improve access to quality, reliable family planning counseling services and contraceptives.Develop, deliver and document innovative, targeted demand creation strategies...With FY 2024 funds, the SHASHA mechanism:.Continues to engage and empower members of parliament and, community and youth advocates to participate in national budget formulation and lobby for meaningful allocations for FP and SRH services.  .Trains public health service providers including community health workers to deliver high quality, FP/SRH services including broadening nursing curricula..Strengthens the private sector role in improving choice and equitable access to high quality FP/SRH services..Engages mobile outreach teams and support private and public sector sites to provide voluntary, free, comprehensive FP counseling and contraceptive services..Enhances accountability and feedback to recipients of care through digital feedback mechanisms, a youth scorecard, and mystery clients..Employs human-centered design-based approaches to generate social and behavior change approaches needed to increase access to and demand for FP among priority populations. ..These activities increase the use of FP services among under-served and poor populations in hard-to-reach geographical locations and those with challenging socio-economic situations such as PWDs, AGYW, and religious objector communities leading to improved maternal and child health outcomes..</narrative>
  </description>
